\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Peru's steady sol  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  may defy emerging markets jinx\b0\par\par\uc2 1,034 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 2012\par 18:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * Peru \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened most in Latin America in 2011\par \par \uc2 * Some say Peru's commodities mix makes it recession-proof\par \par \uc2 By Caroline Stauffer\par \par \uc2 LIMA, Jan 19 (Reuters) - Peru's sol may not repeat its 2011 performance as Latin America's star \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, but strong exports of crisis-proof gold, low \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility and growing confidence in the new president could make it a sound emerging market bet.\par \par \uc2 Local businesses and economists consulted by Reuters mostly see the sol, which gained 4 percent last year, holding stable or strengthening slightly despite the risk of lower commodity prices and fallout from Europe's financial mess.\par \par \uc2 Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been the region's least volatile for years and low volatility makes \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  more attractive for investors looking for less risky bets.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican and Chilean pesos and the Brazilian real , in contrast, each lost about 10 percent in 2011, lashed in the second half of the year when Europe's debt woes drove risk-shy investors out of many emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 The sol outperformed mostly on high prices paid for the commodities it exports, including gold, and got a boost when President Ollanta Humala, a one-time radical leftist, pleasantly surprised investors by appointing conservatives to run economic and monetary policy after taking office in July .\par \par \uc2 "There's a flood of money coming in, both from abroad and internally," said Javier San Miguel, budget manager at Peru's largest consumer goods company \uc2 Alicorp\uc2 . "Exports are still quite good even though commodities prices have come down some. We see a favorable outlook."\par \par \uc2 "Everything Humala has done to date is in line with what the business sector wanted," he added.\par \par \uc2 San Miguel believes the sol will end 2012 up nearly 2 percent from 2011 at 2.65 per U.S. dollar. \uc2 Alicorp\uc2  imports basic food goods like wheat and soy in dollars and sells 75 percent of its finished products domestically in soles.\par \par \uc2 The sol has been trading around its strongest level in more than a decade so far this year, and is 0.15 percent stronger than its 2011 close of 2.96 per U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 RECESSION-PROOF?\par \par \uc2 While many economies in Latin America export commodities, Peru is unique. Unlike neighboring Chile, where copper is dominant and accounts for roughly 50 percent of monthly export revenue, Peru is the world's No. 2 copper and silver producer and Latin America's top gold producer.\par \par \uc2 The dollar value of Peru's monthly gold exports has been nearly equal to that of its copper sales abroad.\par \par \uc2 Ricardo V. Lago, a former World Bank economist, has said that Peru's role as a gold exporter could mean it is virtually recession-proof in a scenario when precious metals prices leap on safe-haven buying in a global economic meltdown.\par \par \uc2 Other economists say even though prices for copper and zinc Peru exports may be softer, prices for its gold and fishmeal are still high. It is the world's top fishmeal producer.\par \par \uc2 "There might be other countries in the region that export gold but it's not as important as in Peru," said Nomura analyst Benito Berber.\par \par \uc2 Peru, one of the region's most open economies, is also an emerging leader in niche agricultural products like asparagus and it has a growing manufacturing sector, mainly textiles. The government forecasts economic growth of around 5.4 percent in 2012, which could be one of the region's fastest rates.\par \par \uc2 "The sol doesn't just depend on commodities. Peru has been more successful in exporting value added goods than other countries in the region and is supported by strong macroeconomic fundamentals," said Mariel Koehler, a trader for Peruvian miner Volcan, the world's No. 4 zinc producer.\par \par \uc2 Koehler sees the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gaining 3.5 percent in 2012 to end at 2.6 per dollar, barring unexpected global economic trauma.\par \par \uc2 $50 BILLION IN THE PIPELINE\par \par \uc2 Others say that while Peru will likely again have the least volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region this year, it is not entirely risk free.\par \par \uc2 Some $50 billion in investment in mining and energy are lined up for the next few years, and some analysts fear the cash will only come through if Humala can avert protests against natural resource extraction in remote areas of the country.\par \par \uc2 U.S.-based Newmont's $4.8 billion copper and gold project has been on hold since locals started protests in northern Cajamarca, demanding the company stop work on the mine for fear it will disrupt scarce water supplies.\par \par \uc2 "The risk for the sol is basically a fall in investment if social conflicts break out in mining areas," said Pedro Tuesta, a Peruvian economist with the 4Cast consultancy, who sees the sol trading between 2.68 and 2.72 at year's end.\par \par \uc2 The sol will likely trade sideways at a time when the Mexican peso could rebound on stronger signs of recovery in the United States, Tuesta said. \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says the Mexican peso and Brazil's real could both outperform the sol in 2012.+\par \par \uc2 But the sol's performance is not entirely determined by market factors. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ample reserves and frequent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have played a role in the sol having a lower volatility. Because \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a small market, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is much more effective at curbing volatility than in Chile or Mexico.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently, purchasing $4.7 billion in 2011, because half of all bank accounts in Peru are held in dollars and exchange rate volatility hurts businesses and consumers, who often get paid in soles and owe debts in dollars.\par \par \uc2 Regardless of how Europe's debt crisis, Chinese demand for minerals and the economic recovery in the United States play out, the sol could well emerge relatively unscathed in 2012.\par \par \uc2 "It will again be the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the lowest volatility," said Nomura's Berber. "And in a scenario in which things continue to complicate in Europe towards mid-year, I see the (sol) depreciating, though less than many other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Ursula Scollo; Editing by Terry Wade and W Simon)\par \par \uc2 PERU-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2 / (ANALYSIS)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120122e81j000jd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
