\par\par \b \uc2 Peru's Sol, Ends 2011 Strong, Continues Roll Into 2012\b0\par\par\uc2 515 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Januar 2012\par 20:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Emerging Markets Report\par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Sol ended 2011 with 4% appreciation against U.S. dollar\par \par \uc2 --Sol continues to post gains in new year\par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars to ease sol strength\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Robert Kozak \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LIMA (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 )--Peru's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the sol, is continuing last year's trend so far in 2012, appreciating against the U.S. dollar and with the Central Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Peru intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to ease those gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The sol has already appreciated 0.15% against the greenback so far this year, ending Tuesday at PEN2.693. That follows a 4% gain last year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The sol has appreciated 14% against the U.S. dollar since the end of 2008, as Peru's economic boom shows few signs of abating and investors flock to the Andean nation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economists say they expect the sol to retain its strength and to continue to appreciate this year, barring any global downturn that would knock the wind out of commodity prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cesar Perez-Novoa, a managing director with Celfin Capital, noted that waning political risk in Peru coupled with strong commodity prices have helped boost the sol in the shorter term, while robust flows of direct foreign investments in the mining, hydrocarbons and utilities sectors will help support the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the medium and longer terms.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The sol should remain relatively stable, with any abrupt gains being met by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the past, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has also met any short-term speculative flows into capital markets with measures aimed at making those investments less attractive.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  however has been continual, with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to buy $3.3 billion in 2011, following the $8.96 billion it bought in 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 So far this year, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has also been robust, with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  entering to buy $242 million on Tuesday. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have driven up \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Peru\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's net international reserves, which ended 2011 at $48.82 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  says it isn't defending any particular exchange rate, only easing volatility.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A recent survey of 24 economic analysts by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , meanwhile, found expectations that the sol will end this year at PEN2.65 to the U.S. dollar and at PEN2.64 to the dollar in 2013.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We believe that the sol will continue to appreciate but that will be less than in 2011," said Roberto Flores, an economist with brokerage Inteligo.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Michael Henderson, an economist with Capital Economics, sees the sol weakening to around PEN2.80 to the dollar by the end of the second quarter, but then to resume appreciating again over the second half, reaching perhaps PEN2.75 to the dollar by year-end.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He pointed to relatively bearish expectations for global demand over the course of this year which will weaken demand for commodity exports and will likely lead to slowing capital inflows into the region as investors shun risk.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Robert Kozak, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \uc2 \f2 ; 51-1-99927 7269; peru@dowjones.com [ 11-01-12 1909GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument EM00000020120111e81b0000o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
