\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Market  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Slightly Weaker, Debt Little Changed\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anjali Cordeiro   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 458 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2011\par 21:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to lose ground in New York trading on Monday, but their losses were less pronounced as investors turned more hopeful that policy makers might be able to engineer a response to the euro-zone's debt problems.\par \par \uc2 In Latin America, the Brazilian real and Mexican peso lost ground against the dollar, but didn't suffer the kind of deep losses they had been experiencing in recent days. Emerging market debt was little changed, with the risk premium, or spread, on the \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  Emerging Markets Bond Index Global two basis points tighter to 460 basis points over Treasurys. In price terms, the index was down 0.87%.\par \par \uc2 "It's primarily due to the external environment.," said Nomura strategist George Lei. "Overall there is some expectation that policy makers will do something on the euro-zone."\par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stands ready to provide the debt-troubled euro zone with additional support if necessary, two of its top officials said Monday, even as they put the onus on governments to act quickly to ward off intensifying debt woes in the region.\par \par \uc2 Still, central banks in growing economies remain concerned about the possible fallouts from a slowdown in the developed world. The Bank of Israel Monday lowered its main interest rate by 25 basis points to 3% to combat the effects of a global slowdown, the latest sign that central banks around the world are shifting to looser monetary policies. The bank pointed to growing expectations that the slowdown in global growth could be worse than expected. The shekel weakened with the dollar rising 0.86% to ILS3.734.\par \par \uc2 Investors were again faced with weak data from the U.S. New-home sales fell for the fourth-straight month in August to the lowest level in a half-year as the bursting of the housing bubble continues to plague the economy. Mexico's economy is particularly closely linked to that of the U.S. The dollar was up 0.43% against the peso to MXN13.6123. Mexico's spread on the Embig was three basis points tighter to 270 basis points over Treasurys. But in price terms, its index was down 1.2%.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in Latin America, the Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Monday to sell $186 million at an average of PEN2.7715 per U.S. dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth out volatility in the exchange market. The peruvian sol was recently up 0.18% to PEN2.772.\par \par \uc2 -By Anjali Cordeiro; Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2200;\par \par \uc2 anjali.cordeiro@dowjones.com [ 26-09-11 1944GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110926e79q0015f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
