\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil, Peru step in to halt  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  plunge\b0\par\par\uc2 914 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2011\par 20:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Brazil sells swaps, Peru trades soles for dollar debt\par \par \uc2 * World growth outlook knocks commodities, LatAm \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Brazil swap auction helps trim loss that reached 4.79 pct\par \par \uc2 (Updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Jeb Blount\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, Sept 22 (Reuters) - Brazil's real trimmed some of its biggest losses since the 2009 world recession and Peru's sol backed away from three-month lows as the countries' central banks moved to bolster their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the face of deteriorating prospects for world growth.\par \par \uc2 In the face of a nearly 5 percent plunge in the real, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $2.75 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the futures market to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <BRBY>. This reversed a tactic used as recently as August to use sell swaps to prevent the real from strengthening.\par \par \uc2 After the swap auction, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebounded, eventually reversing losses. It closed spot market trading at 2 percent weaker than on Wednesday at 1.8946 the real's weakest close in more than two years. [ID:nS1E78L0PK]\par \par \uc2 "The market got the feeling that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to put a range on the real," said Moacir Marcos Junior a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Interbolsa, a Sao Paulo brokerage. "It's like the bank saying, 'look, 1.60 to the dollar, I don't want, but I don't want 1.90 either and I won't let that happen.'"\par \par \uc2 Thursday's declines bring the Brazilian real's losses this month to more than 15 percent, turning what was a nearly 8 percent year-to-date gain in July to 1.52, a 12-year high, into a nearly 15 percent loss.\par \par \uc2 In Peru, after the sol <PEN=PE> fell more than 1 percent to 2.7910, a three-month low, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold 600 million soles ($216.2 million) of readjustable certificates of deposit, or CDRs.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , local banks trade soles with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for dollar-denominated bonds a trade the mimics the sale of dollars and puts pressure on the sol to gain. The sol rebounded to 2.7690, trimming losses to 0.4 percent [ID:nS1E78L0U7]\par \par \uc2 The declines on Thursday came on increasing signs of slowdown in the U.S., Europe and China -- economies responsible for 60 percent of world output. That outlook caused the price of oil, coffee, copper, soybeans and other commodities, which form the bulk of Latin American exports, to fall too. For more information see: [ID:nS1E78L0M6] [ID:nL5E7KM1AQ] [IDnS1E78L0CR]\par \par \uc2 Adding to the gloom, a decision by the U.S. Federal Reserve late Wednesday to launch "\b \highlight22\uc2 Operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  Twist" -- an attempt to cut long-term borrowing rates by shifting Fed holdings of U.S. Treasury debt into longer-term bonds -- was seen as too weak to boost the sluggish U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 In Greece, a 24-hour anti-austerity strike raised doubts about passage of a plan to stave off a Greek debt default that could hobble international banks and cut-off credit and growth world-wide. [ID:nL5E7KM2H3]\par \par \uc2 "We are staring at a possible new global recession and that's pushing investors out of Latin American and other commodities-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and into the dollar," Luciano Rostagno, chief strategist at CM Capital Markets in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 In times of economic stress investors frequently sell assets in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  considered high risk, such as Brazil's real and Mexico's peso, and buy assets priced in dollars, the world's most "liquid" or widely negotiated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso <MXN=D2> weakened 2.83 percent to 14.0701, breaking beyond 14 to the dollar for the first time since April 27, 2009. In contrast to Brazil and \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Mexico's finance minister Jose Antonio Meade said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect a level for the peso would unwise. [ID:nS1E78L1NN]\par \par \uc2 Now that it's weakened beyond 14.0200 to the dollar, it could fall to the 15.59 range, a level last seen at the height of the world recession in March 2009, said Win Thin, an emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 "Emerging markets are getting killed, and Mexico is going to get killed also," he said. COMMODITY PRICES FALL\par \par \uc2 Chile, the major regional economy most dependent on a single commodity, also saw its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunge. The Chilean peso <CLP=CL> weakened 3.91 percent to 520.80 to the dollar after falling as much as 5.06 percent to 527.20, its weakest level since July 21, 2010.\par \par \uc2 The world's largest copper producer earned more than half of export earnings from the base metal, an important component in electrical equipment and electronic goods.\par \par \uc2 Copper <CMCU3> which was already trading at its lowest levels since November after four days of losses plunged for a fifth day, losing 8.19 percent to $7,647.00 a tonne in London, its lowest in more than a year.\par \par \uc2 The Reuters Jefferies commodities index <.CRB>, a measure of 18 key Latin American energy and agricultural commodities such as oil and soybeans, fell 4.41 percent to 307.24, its lowest level since Dec. 1.\par \par \uc2 Colombia, a key producer of oil and coffee and a growing producer of coal, saw its peso <COP2=STFX> fall 2.19 percent to 1,916.05 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Rachel Uranga in Mexico City and Silvio Cascione in Sao Paulo, Editing by Stuart Grudgings and Chizu Nomiyama)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110922e79m001ea\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
