\par\par \b \uc2 EM  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Lose Ground, Debt Spread Slightly Tighter\b0\par\par\uc2 538 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2011\par 22:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Anjali Cordeiro \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Continued worries about global growth held back emerging-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Monday as investors stayed uneasy about taking on fresh risk.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Emerging-market debt was a notch tighter, but Peruvian assets came under pressure after an election victory for a left-wing candidate. The risk premium, or spread, on the \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  Emerging Markets Bond Index Global was three basis points tighter to 309 basis points over Treasurys. In price terms the index was down 0.02%.\par \par \uc2 The victory in Peru by left-wing nationalist Ollanta Humala in Sunday's runoff vote for president raised jitters that he might push for more government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the economy. \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's spread on the Embig was 21 basis points wider to 213 basis points over Treasurys. In price terms the index was down 2.22%.\par \par \uc2 Still, some analysts and investors said it might be some time before Humala makes any radical changes to economic. "He'll be able to do populist things because the economy is doing so well," says John Peta, portfolio manager at Acadian Asset Management. "Some of the most extreme things people have been thinking of probably won't crop up for a while."\par \par \uc2 Early Monday, Peru's sol was a touch weaker after Humala's victory. According to a market observer, the sol was near 2.80 per dollar early Monday, from 2.76 per dollar on Friday. But the market observer said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent too much weakness in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in Latin America, the Chilean economy continues to grow at a brisk pace on the back of healthy industrial production and domestic demand. The country's monthly economic activity index, or Imacec, grew 6.3% on the year in April, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Monday. Chile's spread on the Embig was twenty four basis points tighter to 111 basis points over Treasurys. In price terms the index was up 1.49%.\par \par \uc2 Worries about slowing growth in the U.S. lingered from Friday's weak payrolls data. On Friday, emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Mexican peso pulled back after the U.S. jobs data. That weakness lingered on Monday, with the dollar up 0.70% against the peso to MXN11.7621. The Brazilian real was also weaker with the dollar up 0.32% against the real to BRL1.5845. Brazilian financial analysts and economists reduced their 2011 inflation forecasts for the fifth consecutive week, according to a survey by the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  published Monday. Brazil's spread on the Embig was one basis point tighter to 177 basis points over Treasurys. In price terms, the index was down 0.10%.\par \par \uc2 Outside Latin America, South African Trade Minister Rob Davies said the government is discussing what to do about the rand's strength, which he said is overvalued from unstable global markets and quantitative easing in the U.S. The rand lost ground, with the dollar rising to 1.17% to ZAR6.8074.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Anjali Cordeiro; Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2200; \par    anjjali.cordeiro@dowjones.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Jenny Gross contributed to this article. [ 06-06-11 2011GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110606e766000yf\par }\