\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Peru stocks up on opportunity buys, politics eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 350 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 2011\par 17:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Stocks rise 3.69 pct after finance minister urges calm\par \par \uc2 * Sol \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakens 0.07 percent, cenbank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 (Adds details, commentary)\par \par \uc2 LIMA, April 15 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stock index <.IGRA> gained 3.69 percent on Friday on opportunity buying following a sell-off triggered by worries about the possible victory of a left-wing presidential candidate.\par \par \uc2 The index is still down around 9 percent from last Friday, since left-wing nationalist Ollanta Humala won a first-round vote on Sunday in Peru's presidential race. He faces a tough contest against right-wing populist Keiko Fujimori in a June 5 run-off.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Ismael Benavides pleaded for calm in markets late on Thursday, saying the country's economic fundamentals had never been stronger. He called for candidates to detail their proposals for investors.\par \par \uc2 "Stocks have bounced back following several days of sharp falls and the finance minister has cooled down the panic a bit," said one trader in Lima. "But this is just a pause," he added, signaling more volatility could continue.\par \par \uc2 The country's vast metals sector lures most of its foreign investment and half of the stocks in Lima's bourse are miners. Volcan rose 5 percent while Buenaventura , the largest miner by share value, gained 1.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 Investors are carefully eyeing the campaign and weighing promises from Humala, a former radical, to keep Peru open to investors in one of the world's fastest growing economies. [ID:nN14283353].\par \par \uc2 Stocks have been hurt by fears Humala will boost mining taxes and take over private companies, if elected. Both Fujimori and Humala want to raise taxes on mining firms but Fujimori is considered more business friendly.\par \par \uc2 The local sol \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <PEN=PE> weakened 0.07 percent to bid at 2.323 per dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $91 million on Thursday and has placed 650 million soles ($232 million) in certificates of deposits this week to curb depreciation.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Caroline Stauffer and Ursula Scollo; Editing by Andrew Hay)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-PERU/STOCKS (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110415e74f0018z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
