\par\par \b \uc2 Peru's Main Stock Indexes End Higher; Sol Weakens\b0\par\par\uc2 By Robert Kozak   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 459 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 2011\par 22:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LIMA (Dow Jones)--Peru's main stock market ended higher Thursday, after a roller-coaster ride that at one point saw the stock market's broad index tumble more than 6.0% before recovering.\par \par \uc2 The stock and the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets have become more volatile since Sunday's election that saw left-leaning nationalist leader Ollanta Humala take a first-round victory. Humala is in a runoff vote for president with Congresswoman Keiko Fujimori that will take place June 5.\par \par \uc2 A selloff in the stock market Thursday was deepened by margin calls, analysts said, but once stocks became cheap enough, buyers came back to look for bargains.\par \par \uc2 The Lima Stock Exchange's broad General index ended 0.63% higher at 18,730.84. On Monday, the index fell 3.1%, followed by a 3.58% decline Tuesday and a 6.22% fall Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Peru's other main index, the Selective blue-chip, ended 0.17% higher at 25,859.13 Thursday. The index closed 5.13% lower Wednesday, after losing 3.54% Tuesday and 3.3% Monday.\par \par \uc2 Precious metals miner \uc2 Compania de Minas Buenaventura\uc2  SAA (BVN, BUENAVC1.Vl) ended 9.99% higher Thursday at 110.00 soles ($38.98) on stronger gold prices.\par \par \uc2 Copper miner Sociedad Minera Cerro Verde SAA(CVERDEC1.VL) rose 0.27% to end at $37.50. At one point in the session, Cerro Verde was down more than 14%.\par \par \uc2 Tin miner \uc2 Minsur SA\uc2  (MINSURI1.VL) ended the day 1.26% higher at PEN3.21 on heavy volume.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the sol weakened, ending Thursday's session at PEN2.822 per U.S. dollar. The sol ended the previous session at PEN2.820 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Thursday by selling $91 million at an average of PEN2.8222 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The last time the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars was on Feb. 20, 2009. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth out volatility in the exchange market.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the demand for dollars came mainly from private pension funds, foreign investors and "local agents."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also placed PEN350 million in adjustable certificates of deposit, which inject dollars into the system.\par \par \uc2 The overall amount negotiated during the trading session totaled $635 million.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year purchased $8.96 billion to ease the sol's appreciating trend. It last purchased dollars on Feb. 11 this year.\par \par \uc2 Peru's sol has strengthened by 0.49% against the greenback over a 12-month period.\par \par \uc2 -By Robert Kozak, Dow Jones Newswires; 51 - 99927 7269; peru@dowjones.com [ 04-14-11 1647ET ]\par \par \uc2 1071 1837 5077   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110414e74e000cv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
