\par\par \b \uc2 PREVIEW-Peru  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  seen hiking rate 25 bps to 4 pct\b0\par\par\uc2 352 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 2011\par 22:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  WHAT: Peru's interest rate decision\par \par \uc2  WHEN: Thursday, April 7, around 6 p.m. EST (2300 GMT)\par \par \uc2  OUTCOME: Rate seen rising 25 basis points to 4 pct\par \par \uc2  REUTERS FORECAST: Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday is expected to raise its benchmark interest rate <PECPI=ECI> for a fourth straight month by 25 basis points to tame price increases, according to 13 economists surveyed by Reuters. Ten economists predicted a 25-basis-point hike to 4 percent while three forecast borrowing costs to rise 50 basis points to 4.25 percent. Consumer prices in Peru rose 0.7 percent in March, their fastest pace in nearly three years due to higher food prices. Food and beverages are the largest component of Peru's monthly inflation calculation. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has indicated a "neutral" monetary policy would put the rate at 4.5 percent, suggesting more hikes could be in store.\par \par \uc2  Yet \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Julio Velarde told Reuters on Friday that sharp hikes during unpredictable times are "like putting on the brakes on an icy road" and that maintaining economic stability was crucial as presidential elections approach. For more see [ID:nN01151534].\par \par \uc2  Peruvians go to the polls on April 10 and a run-off vote between the top two candidates is scheduled for June 5.\par \par \uc2  MARKET IMPACT: Seen minimal. Higher interest rates can increase the demand for Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <PEN=PE>, but the sol has recently been more affected by electoral uncertainty. It has weakened around 1 percent against the U.S. dollar in the past two weeks on nationalist candidate Ollanta Humala's gains. Humala now leads four national polls and will likely face one of three opponents, all considered more market friendly, in the second round vote. \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly on the local spot market to prevent sharp volatility that hurts businesses and consumers in a country where half of bank deposits are made in dollars.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Ursula Scollo; Writing by Caroline Stauffer; Editing by James Dalgleish)   \par \par \uc2 PERU-ECONOMY/RATES (PREVIEW)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110408e745001n4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
