\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Peru's sol to shine as cenbank fights inflation\b0\par\par\uc2 672 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 20:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen turning focus to inflation from \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Nomura, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  see sol at 2.6 per dollar by year end\par \par \uc2 * Elections not seen affecting financial markets\par \par \uc2 By Caroline Stauffer\par \par \uc2 LIMA (Reuters) - Peru's sol could be Latin America's strongest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  performer in 2011, appreciating at double the pace of 2010, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  turns its focus to resurgent inflation rather than the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 With Peru's economic growth rate exceeding much of the rest of the world, analysts in Peru and abroad see the sol strengthening to 2.6 or 2.7 per U.S. dollar in 2011, a gain of about 7 percent, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raises interest rates.\par \par \uc2 "We believe the sol will outperform the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the course of the year," said Alejandro Grisanti of \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in New York. "It is a good investment instrument with a strong return and low volatility."\par \par \uc2 Peru's economy is expected to grow 7 percent this year, propelled by strong domestic demand, foreign investment and the country's adherence to budget discipline. It is a far cry from the 1980s when hyper-inflation battered living standards and violent insurgents scared off investors.\par \par \uc2 April's presidential elections will not spook markets as all the frontrunners favor policies that emphasize fiscal restraint, free trade and foreign investment, economists say.\par \par \uc2 Averting \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility was long a goal of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as half of all deposits in the country's banking system are in dollars.\par \par \uc2 Many Peruvians owe loans in dollars but get paid in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , meaning that sharp volatility could hurt consumers and businesses.\par \par \uc2 Now, the threat of external price shocks from food and oil is threatening to push 2011 inflation beyond the 3 percent ceiling of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's annual inflation target.\par \par \uc2 That could prompt the bank to rethink its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well as raise interest rates.\par \par \uc2 "When you have food prices going up -- the most important thing to counterbalance that effect is to let the value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise -- that makes goods cheaper for people," Nomura economist Benito Berber said.\par \par \uc2 Peru raised its benchmark interest rate twice in two months to 3.5 percent to counter rising food and oil prices after three straight months of holding rates steady.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has indicated a "neutral" monetary policy would put the rate at 4.5 percent, suggesting more hikes are in store.\par \par \uc2 "It's already a guideline -- the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is prioritizing inflation over the exchange rate," said Mario Guerrero, an analyst at Scotiabank Peru in Lima.\par \par \uc2 In each of the past two years inflation in Peru was mild or virtually nonexistent, giving the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  space to focus more attention on limiting gains by the sol.\par \par \uc2 On Feb. 10, the day after its most recent interest rate decision, \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $130 million in an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to knock the sol back from a 33-month high.\par \par \uc2 Traders expect the bank to buy between $8 and $9 billion in 2011, similar to the amount purchased in 2010 but not enough to stop the sol from gaining as interest rates rise.\par \par \uc2 "As the sol strengthens, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Gonzalo Navarro, head \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Banco Santander in Lima. "But in the medium term it's not going to change the fundamental tendency of the exchange rate."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's early and aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2010 -- when rock-bottom interest rates in the developed world fed demand for higher yielding emerging market assets -- limited the sol's gains to 2.77 percent.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso in comparison gained around 8 percent that year as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  largely stayed out of the market. But it launched a $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January and many now expect the peso to weaken. (Additional reporting by Ursula Scollo in Lima and Brad Haynes in Santiago; Editing by Andrew Hay)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-PERU/SOL (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110302e7320019m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
